textForEmbedding:
[components-layout-page-index] Page > Usage > Focus on route change

Page — The main content area of a screen, with its title, description, primary action, and the sections beneath them.

On client-side navigation the browser does not reset focus the way a full page load does: it lingers on the link that was clicked, or falls back to `<body>`. Screen-reader and keyboard users then get no signal that the screen changed and are stranded at the top of the tab order. The standard fix is to move focus to the new screen's heading, so the page is announced and the next `Tab` continues from the top of the content.

`usePageFocus` does this. Pass it the current route key (usually the pathname). On each change it makes the page `<h1>` programmatically focusable (`tabIndex={-1}`) and focuses it. The **first render is skipped**, so the initial load is left untouched, and it is a **no-op on a page with no `<h1>`** (an `aria-label`-only `<Page>`).

`<Page>` is router-agnostic and cannot observe navigation on its own, so the route signal comes from your app router (the same [`RouterProvider`](/components/application/routerprovider) you already wire up for links). Call the hook from a component that persists across navigations (the layout / shell level), since the skip-first-render guard is per mount:

```tsx
import { Outlet, useLocation, useNavigate } from 'react-router';
import {
  Page,
  RouterProvider,
  Title,
  usePageFocus,
} from '@marigold/components';

// Renders nothing; it just connects the route signal to the page heading.
const PageFocus = ({ pathname }: { pathname: string }) => {
  usePageFocus(pathname);
  return null;
};

const AppLayout = () => {
  const navigate = useNavigate();
  const { pathname } = useLocation();

  return (
    <RouterProvider navigate={navigate}>
      <Page>
        <PageFocus pathname={pathname} />
        <Page.Header>
          <Title>{/* the current screen's title */}</Title>
        </Page.Header>
        <Outlet />
      </Page>
    </RouterProvider>
  );
};
```

Because the guard is per mount, keep the hook mounted across routes rather than calling it from a screen that unmounts on every navigation, otherwise every route reads as a first render and focus never moves.

The heading lookup happens synchronously when the effect runs, so the `<h1>` must already be in the DOM at that point. If a route's `<Title>` renders behind a `React.lazy` + `<Suspense>` boundary, it can still be loading when the effect fires, and focus silently never moves. Hoist the title (or the route signal) above the Suspense boundary for routes that lazy-load their content.

